\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Implications for deployment.}
A uniform compression budget implicitly accepts order-of-magnitude severity penalties during low-sparsity reasoning phases. Pilot evaluation ($n{=}30$, $s{=}0.25$; Appendix~\ref{app:pilot-mitigation}) achieved 18\% oracle recovery (95\% CI: $[-39\%, 45.8\%]$), falling below the pre-registered 30\% gate; the CI spans zero, indicating no statistically distinguishable improvement. Effective phase-aware allocation likely requires finer phase boundary estimation than current proxies provide. Additional mechanisms (e.g., strategy switching, adaptive thresholding) warrant investigation. We hypothesize that phase-modulated severity may extend to alternative attention-mass-based scoring methods (H$_2$O \cite{h2o-neurips2023}, SnapKV \cite{snapkv-neurips2024}, PyramidKV \cite{pyramidkv-colm2025}), which remains an open question for future work. Extended discussion in Appendix~\ref{app:extended-discussion}.

\paragraph{Diagnostic framework value.}
While we do not propose a new compression method, the phase-gated analysis framework has direct engineering utility: (1)~the Interaction Index can serve as a per-phase evaluation metric for assessing the phase robustness of any new compound compression scheme; (2)~the dose-response curves (Figure~\ref{fig:hero}) provide target operating points for phase-aware controllers---e.g., maintaining $s \leq 0.20$ during low-sparsity phases to keep $\Delta < 1.5$; (3)~the strategy specificity finding carries a concrete engineering implication: importance-based eviction under compound sparsity requires phase-aware protection that random eviction does not, because the interaction is structurally coupled to the scoring mechanism rather than to compression magnitude alone.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental-setup}

\paragraph{Models.}
We evaluate on two 7B-parameter models sharing the Qwen2 backbone (28 layers, 28 heads, 4-group GQA): \textbf{DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B}~\cite{deepseek-r1-2025}, distilled from DeepSeek-R1 with explicit chain-of-thought traces yielding pronounced phase structure; and \textbf{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct}~\cite{qwen25-2024}, with standard instruction tuning, providing a cross-training-paradigm replication target. Both are loaded in \texttt{bfloat16} with eager attention.\footnote{An earlier \texttt{float16} configuration produced sporadic NaN values; \texttt{bfloat16} eliminated all occurrences.}

\paragraph{Dataset.}
We evaluate all 500 problems from MATH-500~\cite{math500-neurips2021}. Each problem is decoded greedily (\texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048). Problems with degenerate phase assignments (null low or high phase) are excluded, yielding effective sample sizes of $n{=}496$ for DeepSeek (4 excluded) and $n{=}475$ for Qwen (25 excluded). Qwen exhibits a higher mid-phase null rate (45.2\% vs.\ 10.2\% for DeepSeek) due to lower temporal autocorrelation in its attention sparsity patterns (lag-1 $r{=}0.718$ vs.\ $0.894$, $d{=}-1.68$); this does not affect the primary gradient metric $\Delta = \mathrm{II}_{\textit{low}} - \mathrm{II}_{\textit{high}}$, which requires only low and high phases. Sensitivity analysis across $\texttt{min\_phase\_length} \in \{5, 10, 20\}$ supports robustness (Appendix~\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Sparsity configuration.}
We apply symmetric compound sparsity at $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$: at each level, the head pruning rate $p{=}s$ (fraction of heads removed per layer) equals the KV cache eviction rate $e{=}s$ (fraction of positions evicted). We compute perplexity under four conditions: dense, attention-only ($p{=}s, e{=}0$), KV-only ($p{=}0, e{=}s$), and compound ($p{=}s, e{=}s$). We compare \emph{importance-based} eviction (position-normalized cumulative attention score, Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance}; BOS always retained) and \emph{random} eviction (uniform). Heads are pruned per layer by Frobenius norm; under random eviction, both heads and positions are selected uniformly.

\paragraph{Phase segmentation and metrics.}
Each sequence is segmented into three phases using the quantile-based method (\S\ref{par:phase-segmentation}), with $\texttt{min\_phase\_length}{=}10$. We compute segment-level perplexity and II (Eq.~\ref{eq:ii}), reporting medians due to heavy right-skew (mean/median ratios up to 43:1). The amplification ratio $G$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:gradient}) is reported in Appendix~\ref{app:g-metric}. Robustness to segmentation granularity (quartile, quintile; Appendix~\ref{app:seg-robust}) and minimum phase length ($5$, $10$, $20$ tokens; Appendix~\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}) is verified.

\paragraph{Segmentation validation.}
Quantile-based segmentation shows strong agreement with model-intrinsic \texttt{<think>} boundaries on DeepSeek (Cohen's $\kappa{=}0.89$, token-level accuracy $94.2\%$, AUC-ROC${=}0.98$; $n{=}48$, GSM8K): $57.8\%$ of \texttt{<think>} tokens fall in the low-sparsity phase and $62.7\%$ of answer tokens fall in the high-sparsity phase, confirming that attention-sparsity phases recover genuine reasoning structure without requiring explicit delimiters. Boundary proximity is tight (median 1 token; $81.2\%$ within 10 tokens). Appendix~\ref{app:think-validation} provides full details.

\paragraph{Statistical analysis.}
\label{par:stats}
All reported confidence intervals use nonparametric bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$, seed$=$42)~\cite{efron-1993}. Significance tests use Wilcoxon signed-rank (paired within-model) or Mann-Whitney $U$ (cross-training-paradigm), with Bonferroni correction at family-wise $k{=}4$ (per model$\times$strategy combination, 4 sparsity levels testing one dose-response hypothesis): each family tests a single directional question (``does $\Delta$ increase with $s$?'') under fixed model and strategy, making $k{=}4$ the natural grouping. Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction across all 44 tests is reported as a robustness check in Appendix~\ref{app:bh-fdr}; all Bonferroni-significant results remain significant under BH-FDR. Dose-response monotonicity is supported by the Jonckheere-Terpstra test ($z{=}6.2$--$33.7$, all $p < 10^{-9}$), which uses per-problem values (${\sim}496$ per group) rather than aggregated medians.\footnote{Spearman rank correlation on the four aggregate medians ($\rho{=}1.0$, $n{=}4$; interpret with caution given the small number of levels) is consistent; we rely on the JT test as primary evidence.} Scaling from $n{=}50$ (profiling) to $n{=}500$ narrowed bootstrap CI widths by $2.5$--$5.9{\times}$ across all 16 conditions, showing precision gains. The paired median difference $\Delta$ is the primary metric; $G$ (amplification ratio, Eq.~\ref{eq:gradient}) is reported in Appendix~\ref{app:g-metric} as an auxiliary illustrative ratio.\footnote{Experiments on 4$\times$ professional GPUs with 48\,GB memory.}

% Section 3: Background and Problem Setup
% Note: eq:ii and eq:gradient are the canonical definitions.
% Full formulation details in Appendix (app:formulation-details).

\section{Background and Problem Setup}
\label{sec:background}

We formalize compound sparsity, define the Interaction Index, and introduce phase segmentation. Full mathematical details appear in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}
Let $s \in (0,1)$ denote a unified sparsity budget. \emph{Attention head pruning} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of heads per layer by Frobenius norm importance \cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}. \emph{KV cache eviction} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of token positions from the cache. We compare two strategies: \emph{importance-based}, which scores positions by position-normalized cumulative attention mass (Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance} in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}), and \emph{random}, which evicts uniformly at random \cite{nacl-acl2024}. Applying both simultaneously yields \emph{compound sparsity}; the two axes interact because head pruning alters the importance ranking used for eviction \cite{spark-aaai2026}. Existing joint-pruning approaches such as GRASPrune \cite{graspune-acl2026} learn global budget-constrained gates but treat decisions as static across generation.

\paragraph{Interaction Index.}
For a text segment, let $\text{PPL}_c$ denote perplexity under condition $c \in \{d, a, k, b\}$ (dense, attention-only, KV-only, compound). Normalizing by the dense baseline yields $r_c = \text{PPL}_c / \text{PPL}_d$, and:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{II} = r_b - r_a - r_k + 1.
    \label{eq:ii}
\end{equation}
$\mathrm{II} > 0$ indicates super-additive degradation; $\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive independence. We adopt the additive null as primary: it assumes $r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$ (compound loss equals sum of individual losses), the most conservative independence assumption. Although the two axes are mechanistically linked (\S\ref{sec:analysis}), rejection ($\mathrm{II} > 0$) establishes super-additivity regardless of null specification. Appendix~\ref{app:multiplicative-null} reports parallel results under the multiplicative null ($r_b = r_a \cdot r_k$); the phase-severity gradient is preserved ($G_{\times}{=}21$--$64{\times}$), supporting null-choice invariance. The II distribution is heavily right-skewed; we report medians throughout.

\paragraph{Severity gradient.}
To capture phase variation, the per-problem \emph{amplification ratio} is:
\begin{equation}
    G^{(i)} = \frac{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low}}}{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{high}}},
    \label{eq:gradient}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low/high}}$ is the interaction index in the low/high-sparsity phase for problem $i$. We report $G = \widetilde{G^{(i)}}$, the median across problems, with bootstrap CIs. We use the median of per-problem ratios (not the ratio of medians) to preserve per-problem pairing; see Appendix~\ref{app:profiling-subset} for comparison. $G$ serves as an auxiliary descriptive ratio that illustrates the degree of phase concentration; the primary statistical evidence for the severity gradient rests on $\Delta$ and paired Wilcoxon tests (\S\ref{sec:phase-amplification}).

\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}
\label{par:phase-segmentation}
Inspired by ThinKV's phase-aware perspective on reasoning-time compression~\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}, each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, using a quantile-based segmentation rather than explicit reasoning delimiters (e.g., \texttt{<think>} tokens). Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). We note that this segmentation is an \emph{operationalization choice}: attention sparsity serves as a continuous proxy for reasoning phase rather than a theoretically grounded boundary. We validate robustness to segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile; Appendix~\ref{app:seg-robust}) and minimum phase length (Appendix~\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}). Validation against DeepSeek's explicit \texttt{<think>} markers using adaptive per-sequence quantile segmentation yields substantial agreement ($\kappa{=}0.89$, AUC-ROC${=}0.98$; $n{=}48$; Appendix~\ref{app:think-validation}). Importantly, phases reflect genuine computational mode differences even under the dense (uncompressed) baseline: per-phase dense perplexity increases monotonically from high ($1.10$) through mid ($1.17$) to low ($1.18$), confirming that low-sparsity phases correspond to inherently harder computation; the phase gradient in compound severity (\S\ref{sec:results}) thus amplifies a pre-existing computational asymmetry rather than creating one. Details in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

